WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:01.220
How Hong Kong Built the World's Best Transit

00:00:01.220 --> 00:00:02.750
This video is sponsored by Brilliant.

00:00:02.750 --> 00:00:08.200
The first 200 to use the link in the description
get 20% off the annual subscription.

00:00:08.200 --> 00:00:14.300
On any given weekday, 5.9 million people take
Hong Kong’s public transportation.

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:19.999
Of them, 5.894 million, or 99.9% arrive on
time.

00:00:19.999 --> 00:00:25.890
It’s so efficient, so clean and so convenient
that virtually everyone uses it.

00:00:25.890 --> 00:00:30.380
If the U.S., with 800 cars per one thousand
people, has a car culture,

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:34.770
Hong Kong, with only 92, has a culture of
public transit.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:39.300
With the exception of the outlying islands,
there's an easy way to get almost anywhere

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:40.700
in the territory…

00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:46.050
On Hong Kong Island, the iconic and only fully
double-decker tram system in the world costs

00:00:46.050 --> 00:00:50.620
just 2.3 Hong Kong Dollars, or 30 US Cents.

00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:55.650
Nearby, the Peak Tram takes you to the most
spectacular view of the city in just 7 minutes

00:00:55.650 --> 00:01:01.890
- a steep 370-meter, or 12,000-foot ascent
that would otherwise take a full hour.

00:01:01.890 --> 00:01:07.200
You can cross over to Kowloon on the relaxing
120-year old Star Ferry.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:11.010
And when you leave Hong Kong, there’s no
need to take your bags to the Airport.

00:01:11.010 --> 00:01:14.960
You can drop them in the city, then shop,
eat, explore, and they’ll automatically

00:01:14.960 --> 00:01:20.060
be sent to the airport, loaded onto your plane,
and be there waiting for you wherever you

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:21.060
arrive.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.620
There are busses, cable-cars, helicopters,
and the longest outdoor covered escalator

00:01:25.620 --> 00:01:27.710
in the world.

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:33.159
Finally, the 12-line, 93-station Mass Transit
Railway, or M-T-R, is responsible for moving

00:01:33.159 --> 00:01:36.180
most of the city’s seven and a half million
people.

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:40.860
A special Mickey Mouse themed line goes to
Disneyland, another to the racehorse on event

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:44.020
days, and yet another crosses over into mainland
China.

00:01:44.020 --> 00:01:49.280
It’s no wonder more people take the MTR
every single day than the entire population

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:51.480
of Norway or Singapore.

00:01:51.480 --> 00:01:55.920
But unlike nearly every other place in the
world, Hong Kong’s public transit pays for

00:01:55.920 --> 00:01:56.920
itself.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:02.810
It’s so profitable that it actually subsidizes
the government - not the other way around.

00:02:02.810 --> 00:02:10.170
And its unique business model is an unexpected
window into the city’s ongoing protests.

00:02:10.170 --> 00:02:14.200
In most cities around the world, transit is
seen as a public good.

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:19.709
Like building roads or minting coins, busses
and trains are not expected to be profitable.

00:02:19.709 --> 00:02:23.610
And while it’s economically useful to get
people to and from where they can make and

00:02:23.610 --> 00:02:28.060
spend money, there’s less incentive to make
it clean, quick or comfortable.

00:02:28.060 --> 00:02:33.640
The problem is, especially in America, users
of public transit are disproportionately young

00:02:33.640 --> 00:02:34.670
and poor.

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:37.140
Which is to say, not politically useful.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:42.950
In Tucson, Arizona, for example, every third
rider of public transportation is in poverty,

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:46.320
compared to 13% in the general population.

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.989
Because of this, it’s treated like social
welfare - mostly for the poor and subsidized

00:02:50.989 --> 00:02:52.210
by everyone.

00:02:52.210 --> 00:02:56.310
The simple way to measure how much money is
a system loses is to calculate its Farebox

00:02:56.310 --> 00:03:01.310
Recovery Ratio - that is, the total amount
made from rides divided by the cost to run

00:03:01.310 --> 00:03:02.310
it.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:08.020
The lowest, meaning least profitable cities
are almost all in the U.S.: Detroit is 20%,

00:03:08.020 --> 00:03:11.770
Dallas is 14, and Santa Clara County is 10!

00:03:11.770 --> 00:03:15.940
In other words, tickets only pay for one-tenth
of its expenses.

00:03:15.940 --> 00:03:21.690
The other 90% is subsidized, reluctantly,
by local, state, and federal governments.

00:03:21.690 --> 00:03:27.769
An example of a high ratio is San Francisco's
Bay Area Rapid Transit, at 70% - about the

00:03:27.769 --> 00:03:31.270
same as Berlin, Beijing, or Amsterdam.

00:03:31.270 --> 00:03:34.200
But then there’s Hong Kong’s MTR.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:40.050
Fares pay for 185% of its operating expenses,
the highest in the world.

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:45.560
In 2017, it generated 21.4 Billion Hong Kong
Dollars of profit.

00:03:45.560 --> 00:03:48.769
And yet, somehow, it’s still extremely affordable.

00:03:48.769 --> 00:03:53.020
To get from one side of Hong Kong Island to
the other - from Chai Wan to Kennedy town

00:03:53.020 --> 00:03:58.239
- costs just 10.7 HKD or 1.3 US Dollars.

00:03:58.239 --> 00:04:05.019
To go all the way from the border with mainland
China to Central is 50.5, or 6.4 US Dollars.

00:04:05.019 --> 00:04:09.940
It’s about half that for children, students,
the elderly, and disabled.

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:14.270
There are some obvious reasons Hong Kong has
such a big transportation advantage.

00:04:14.270 --> 00:04:21.580
First, as one of the densest places on earth,
with, for example, 57,250 people per square

00:04:21.580 --> 00:04:26.770
kilometer, or 22,104 per square mile in the
Kwun Tong district.

00:04:26.770 --> 00:04:29.840
Second, its infrastructure is relatively young.

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:36.530
The London Underground opened in 1863, the
New York City Subway in 1904, and Hong Kong’s

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:38.919
MTR in 1979.

00:04:38.919 --> 00:04:42.320
But there’s another secret ingredient to
its success.

00:04:42.320 --> 00:04:48.120
The MTR is so profitable despite being so
affordable by selling to businesses its biggest

00:04:48.120 --> 00:04:52.949
asset: access to the 90% of Hong Kong’s
population that takes the train.

00:04:52.949 --> 00:04:58.510
The MTR’s real product is its ability to
almost unilaterally decide where Hong Kongers

00:04:58.510 --> 00:04:59.510
go.

00:04:59.510 --> 00:05:01.080
Here’s how it works:

00:05:01.080 --> 00:05:05.449
First, before construction takes place, the
Hong Kong SAR government grants the company

00:05:05.449 --> 00:05:08.080
development rights around new stations.

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:13.760
The MTR pays for the land, but at “greenfield”
prices - meaning, its value before the station

00:05:13.760 --> 00:05:14.760
is built.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:19.430
Then, it builds the new line or station, which,
because of the MTR’s ubiquity, instantly

00:05:19.430 --> 00:05:22.630
makes the land in and around it much more
valuable.

00:05:22.630 --> 00:05:27.020
Finally, it sells or leases that space to
property developers.

00:05:27.020 --> 00:05:29.900
Over 1,400 shops are leased inside stations,

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:35.520
and the areas above and around them are often
partly-owned by or in a profit-sharing agreement

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:36.830
with the MTR.

00:05:36.830 --> 00:05:42.250
This includes the first and second tallest
buildings in Hong Kong, the Four Seasons Hotel,

00:05:42.250 --> 00:05:45.170
the Ritz Carlton, and about 50 others.

00:05:45.170 --> 00:05:48.970
It also attaches MTR-branded malls to popular
stations.

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:56.000
There’s even a 50-high rise, 21,000 apartment,
MTR neighborhood called LOHAS park.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:00.460
Because of this, it’s quite possible to
unknowingly spend your entire day - sleeping,

00:06:00.460 --> 00:06:05.030
eating, shopping, watching a film - without
technically leaving public transit.

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:10.570
It’s called the Rail + Property strategy
and, predictably, it’s very profitable.

00:06:10.570 --> 00:06:17.190
In 2018, the company made 8.2 billion HKD
from transit operations - meaning fares - but

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:21.400
12.7 from property development, rent, and
commercial business.

00:06:21.400 --> 00:06:26.050
It then gives some of this money back, as
stock dividends, to the government - who owns

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:28.170
75% of the company.

00:06:28.170 --> 00:06:33.520
By not relying on government funding, the
MTR can maintain and grow its operations quickly,

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:35.790
as an efficient, market-based company.

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:40.669
The government, meanwhile, sees transit not
as an insatiable money pit but an important

00:06:40.669 --> 00:06:44.460
source of income that it’s actively incentivized
to improve.

00:06:44.460 --> 00:06:49.500
For example, the MTR is legally required to
report any delay longer than 8 minutes to

00:06:49.500 --> 00:06:50.500
the government.

00:06:50.500 --> 00:06:55.050
And a 31-minute delay results in a 1 million
HKD fine.

00:06:55.050 --> 00:06:58.730
Trains are quiet and clean - with a strict
no eating or drinking policy.

00:06:58.730 --> 00:07:02.880
They arrive so often that there is no need
for a timetable.

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:08.110
The Octopus card makes paying as easy as a
single tap - and it also works in convenience

00:07:08.110 --> 00:07:14.530
stores, supermarkets, restaurants, vending
machines, laundry machines, even parking meters.

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:19.930
Finally, 75% of the population lives within
one kilometer of a station.

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:23.229
As great as it is, though, there is a catch.

00:07:23.229 --> 00:07:27.710
Looking at a map, Hong Kong looks like a tiny
place for 7.5 million people.

00:07:27.710 --> 00:07:32.850
But, behind the rows of skyscrapers, visible
from almost anywhere in the territory, are

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.449
luscious, green, undeveloped mountains.

00:07:35.449 --> 00:07:40.160
Hong Kong is very dense, but the problem isn’t
a lack of space,

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:43.110
Almost three-quarters of its land is green
space.

00:07:43.110 --> 00:07:48.360
Only 25% is even developed and a tiny 7% is
residential.

00:07:48.360 --> 00:07:53.050
Of course, some of this land is mountainous
and simply can’t be developed but a significant

00:07:53.050 --> 00:07:56.220
portion sits un-used or under-used.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:08:02.650
For example, the sprawling 420-acre Fanling
golf course is surrounded by rows of cramped

00:08:02.650 --> 00:08:04.360
40-floor residential buildings.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:08.810
There’s a simple economic reason housing
is in such short supply.

00:08:08.810 --> 00:08:13.500
To attract businesses, Hong Kong has one of
the lowest income tax rates in the world.

00:08:13.500 --> 00:08:16.509
Instead, it makes money from land leases.

00:08:16.509 --> 00:08:20.620
Aside from St. John’s Cathedral in Central
which is privately owned by the Church of

00:08:20.620 --> 00:08:26.940
England, and a few 999-year leases like the
US Consulate, all land in Hong Kong is government

00:08:26.940 --> 00:08:30.330
owned and leased - usually for 50 years.

00:08:30.330 --> 00:08:35.050
27% of government revenue comes from land
sales, which means it has little economic

00:08:35.050 --> 00:08:39.199
reason to flood the market with more supply,
despite the intense need.

00:08:39.199 --> 00:08:44.509
Instead, it constrains supply by slowly auctioning
off land while housing prices continue to

00:08:44.509 --> 00:08:45.509
skyrocket.

00:08:45.509 --> 00:08:53.410
A tiny 200 square foot studio may cost 4 or
5 million HKD or 600,000 US dollars.

00:08:53.410 --> 00:08:57.300
The median annual salary is worth about 12
square feet.

00:08:57.300 --> 00:09:02.089
In this way, the MTR corporation and the government,
with their perverse incentives to restrict

00:09:02.089 --> 00:09:07.050
land rights and drive up prices, are, in part
responsible for a housing crisis in which

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:11.970
Hong Kongers live in tiny, dirty homes sometimes
about the size of coffins.

00:09:11.970 --> 00:09:16.949
Thus, one way or another, Hong Kongers pay
for their world-class public transit.

00:09:16.949 --> 00:09:21.480
Not only in the form of inaccessible housing
but also in the control they inadvertently

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:27.499
grant the MTR, and, by extension, the government,
in deciding where and when they can travel.

00:09:27.499 --> 00:09:32.199
While fast, cheap, and efficient transit has
enabled millions of Hong Kongers to fight

00:09:32.199 --> 00:09:36.769
for their freedom, it has more recently become
a tool to hinder them.

00:09:36.769 --> 00:09:41.480
Stations in and around protests are regularly
closed during the weekends, effectively cutting

00:09:41.480 --> 00:09:46.569
off most of the population from arriving and
conveniently pinning the blame on protestors

00:09:46.569 --> 00:09:47.569
for the interruptions.

00:09:47.569 --> 00:09:53.619
It’s a cautionary tale about how indisputably-good
technology can easily be co-opted in the wrong

00:09:53.619 --> 00:09:54.829
hands.

00:09:54.829 --> 00:09:57.670
The solution, though, is better technology.

00:09:57.670 --> 00:10:02.329
When government forces spy on the apps protestors
use to communicate, the solution is to build

00:10:02.329 --> 00:10:05.230
a better, more secure and encrypted system.

00:10:05.230 --> 00:10:08.920
To protect against facial recognition, we
need to better understand how their neural

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:11.340
networks and machine learning algorithms work.

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:17.760
